{
  "dom_id": "step-q76978",
  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "Given that $7≡19\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n),$ which of the following could be the value of $n?$\n\n- $n=5$\n- $n=6$\n- $n=7$",
  "worked_solution": "First, we recall that \n\n$$\na≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,n∣(a-b).\n$$\n\nSo, $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$ if and only if $(a-b)$ is divisible by $n$ (there is no remainder).\n\nWe have $a-b=7-19=-12.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Note that $5∤(-12).$ Therefore, the statement is false when $n=5.$\n\n- Note that $6∣(-12)$ because $(-12)÷6=-2,$ and there is no remainder. Therefore, the statement is true when $n=6.$\n\n- Note that $7∤(-12).$ Therefore, the statement is false when $n=7.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
